This product contains code and design derived from the following
Apache-2.0 licensed projects:

  Tianshu-harness (https://github.com/huiliyi37/Tianshu-harness)
  Copyright (c) Tianshu-harness contributors
  Derived works:
    - lib/verify.js
        The idea of a repository declaring "touch X, therefore run Y" as data
        rather than prose, the glob-to-command route shape, and the four
        command kinds (test / build / typecheck / lint). The trust gate that
        design needed is deliberately absent here because the capability it
        gated — spawning the declared command — is also absent; see the file
        header for why.
    - lib/remind.js
        The rule that a reminder must name files and commands rather than
        advise a behaviour ("a reminder that says 'consider verifying your
        changes' is worse than nothing"), and the per-block character budget.
    - lib/pheromone.js
        Decaying, reinforcing per-path signals shared across sessions, the
        additive reinforcement rule, and the half-life table.
    - lib/index.js
        The `tools/post-execute` listener shape that prepends an
        `additionalContexts` notice, and the reminder digest that suppresses a
        repeat injection within one turn.

  dsh-context-diet (https://github.com/SOLECA794/dsh-context-diet)
  Copyright (c) dsh-context-diet contributors
  Derived works:
    - scripts/verify-apply.mjs and tests/stubs/dsh-tools.mjs
        The module-hook stub pattern that lets `apply()` be exercised without
        a DSH install, and the `check(label, fn)` reporting harness.

  DeepSeek Harness (bundled `app.asar`)
  Derived works:
    - lib/index.js
        The injected-notice message shape (`role: 'user'`, a labeled `source`
        with `form: 'notice'` and a bounded `summary`), modelled on the
        harness's own `repeat-tool-reminder` guard so an unlabeled context
        cannot render as a user prompt in derived history.

No code was copied verbatim from the projects above; the derivations are
re-implementations of their published mechanisms, with the algorithms and
threshold values preserved so the behaviour can be compared.
